# Target C-II — count-optimal adjacent Pareto refinement forces at most `1762` switching rows

**Date:** 2026-09-15  
**Status:** proof-only source-level structural strengthening  
**Scope:** the audited `t=(q-k)/D<=1.844` full-core Type-II adjacent-cut backbone  
**Dependencies:** `target_c_adjacent_cut_pareto_refinement_2026-09-15.md`  
**Certificate:** `research/verify_target_c_adjacent_cut_pareto_1763.py`  
**LEVEL I STATUS:** `STILL OPEN. C-II REMAINS OPEN.`

The general Pareto inequality from the adjacent-cut refinement says that fixed positive
thresholds `n,b,a` are simultaneously attainable on one internal boundary whenever

\[
{2\pi\over n}
+{2\over b}
+{2C_{phase}\over d_{low}a}
<p,
\]

where

\[
p={278778993\over78216868750}=0.0035641799\ldots,
\qquad
d_{low}={9891\over62500}.
\]

The earlier convenient choice `n=1800`, `a=b=10^6` leaves visible numerical room.  If
the downstream objective is instead to minimize the number of exceptional diameter
rows, that choice can be driven almost to the limit of the switch-count budget itself.

Take

\[
\boxed{
n=1763,
\qquad a=b=3\times10^8.}
\]

Using the same outward rational bounds

\[
\pi<{355\over113},
\qquad
C_{phase}
<{3258689123681\over612912000000},
\]

the exact verifier gives

\[
{2(355/113)\over1763}
=0.00356391709626\ldots,
\]

\[
{2\over3\times10^8}
=6.6666\ldots\times10^{-9},
\]

and

\[
{2C_{phase}^{up}\over d_{low}(3\times10^8)}
=2.2397181\ldots\times10^{-7}.
\]

Their sum is

\[
0.00356414773474\ldots
<p,
\]

with exact rational gap

\[
\boxed{>3\times10^{-8}.}
\]

Therefore one adjacent boundary satisfies

\[
\boxed{N_i<1763+o(1),}
\]

\[
\boxed{DB_i<3\times10^8+o(1),}
\]

and

\[
\boxed{D(q_i+q_{i+1})<3\times10^8+o(1).}
\]

Because `N_i` is integer, along every sufficiently large survivor this yields

\[
\boxed{N_i\le1762.}
\]

The other two bounds still imply

\[
B_i=O(D^{-1}),
\qquad
d_{\mathbb{RP}^1}(u_i,u_{i+1})=O(D^{-1}),
\]

with fixed constants, so the exact opposite-shear finite-cusp reduction remains valid.
Combining with
`target_c_adjacent_switching_caterpillar_2026-09-15.md`, the large-first-derivative
exceptional graph is therefore a caterpillar forest with at most `1762` edges for all
sufficiently large members of the live branch.

This is essentially count-optimal for the current three-budget argument.  Indeed the
switch-count budget alone has limiting average threshold

\[
{2(355/113)\over p}
=1762.8699958\ldots .
\]

Thus reducing the guaranteed exceptional count below `1762` requires genuinely new
information beyond the present global switch-count, switching-weight, and phase-defect
budgets.

**Disposition:** proved proof-only count-focused Pareto endpoint for the adjacent
finite-cusp normal form. C-II and Level I remain open.
